/-!
# The e-variables of an i.i.d. sample

A function `H` of `n` observations is an e-variable for the null of the i.i.d. laws
`{P ^ n | P a probability measure}` when its expectation under every i.i.d. law is at most `1`.
This file describes these e-variables exactly [EVR26, Note 11].

* **Any observation space.** `H` is such an e-variable exactly when it is measurable and every
  V-statistic of `H` is at most `1`: for every finite sample `y₁, …, y_M`,
  `M⁻ⁿ ∑_{i ∈ [M]ⁿ} H (y_{i₁}, …, y_{iₙ}) ≤ 1`. The V-statistic is the expectation of `H` under the
  i.i.d. law of the empirical measure of the sample (Tau Ceti's
  `TauCeti.Probability.empiricalMeasureOfFintype`), which gives one direction; for the other, the
  expectation of the V-statistic under `P ^ M` is at least `M (M - 1) ⋯ (M - n + 1) / Mⁿ` times
  the expectation of `H` under `P ^ n`, since `H ≥ 0`, and this factor tends to `1`.
* **A finite alphabet.** The expectation of `H` under the i.i.d. law of a probability vector `u`
  is the polynomial `∑ e, (∏ i, u (e i)) * H e`, so the e-variables are the functions for which
  this polynomial is at most `1` on the simplex.
* **Two observations on a finite alphabet.** `H` is an e-variable exactly when it is finite and
  `J - A_H` is a copositive matrix, where `J` is the matrix of ones and `A_H` is the matrix of the
  values of `H`: `w ⬝ᵥ A_H *ᵥ w ≤ (∑ v, w v) ^ 2` for every nonnegative vector `w`. The quadratic
  form only sees the symmetrization of `A_H`.
* **Graphs.** For a finite graph with clique number `ω ≥ 1`, `c` times the indicator that the two
  observations are adjacent is an e-variable exactly when `c ≤ ω / (ω - 1)`, by the
  Motzkin–Straus theorem (`SimpleGraph.dotProduct_adjMatrix_mulVec_le`). Deciding whether a
  function of two i.i.d. observations is an e-variable therefore decides whether a graph has a
  clique of a given size.

## Main statements

* `ProbabilityTheory.isEVar_pi_iff_sum_comp_le_one`: the V-statistic criterion, for any finite
  set of observations on any measurable space.
* `ProbabilityTheory.isEVar_pi_iff_sum_prod_mul_le_one`: the criterion on a finite alphabet.
* `ProbabilityTheory.isEVar_pi_finTwo_iff_dotProduct_mulVec_le`: the copositivity criterion for
  two observations.
* `ProbabilityTheory.isEVar_mul_indicator_adj_iff`: the e-variables `c * 𝟙{x₀ ~ x₁}` of a graph
  [EVR26, Note 11, Theorem 4].

## References

* [EVR26] *Answers to open questions in e-values and anytime-valid inference*, Note 11, and its
  Lean library `EValuesResearch`, github.com/RemyDegenne/evalues_research, 2026, whose proof of
  [EVR26, Note 11, Theorem 4] is adapted here.
* [LRR26] M. Larsson, A. Ramdas and J. Ruf, *Testing hypotheses generated by constraints*, Math.
  Oper. Res. (2026), arXiv:2504.02974v5, §10, which asks for a description of these e-variables.
-/

public section

open MeasureTheory Matrix Finset Filter Topology TauCeti.Probability
